{"name":"Buoyant","hex":"#65707e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buoyant","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having buoyancy; able to float.","Lighthearted and lively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buoyant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buoyancy","blocks":[{"text":"Buoyancy, or upthrust, is the force exerted by a fluid opposing the weight of a partially or fully immersed object. The pressure of a fluid increases with depth below the fluid's surface because of the weight of the fluid above. Thus, the pressure at the bottom of a container of fluid is greater than at the top of the container. Similarly, the pressure at the bottom of an object submerged in a fluid is greater than at the top of the object. This pressure difference results in a net upward force on the object. The magnitude of the force is proportional to the pressure difference, and, as explained by Archimedes' principle, is equivalent to the weight of the fluid that would otherwise occupy the submerged volume of the object, i.e. the displaced fluid."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Buoyancy.svg/330px-Buoyancy.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buoyancy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/1280px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A metallic coin (an old British pound coin) floats in mercury due to the buoyancy force upon it and appears to float higher because of the surface tension of the mercury.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/500px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/1280px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Canard_Colvert_02.jpg/500px-Canard_Colvert_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A duck has difficulties to get under water due to its buoyancy. When no swimming forces are implied, the natural equilibrium of forces keeps about half of the duck above water. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanard_Colvert_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/1280px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Pressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pressure distribution on an immersed cube","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APressure_distribution_on_an_immersed_cube.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Forces_on_an_immersed_cube.png/500px-Forces_on_an_immersed_